> Nonsingular full matrix, singular leading block

The row(1,1,0) defines the matrix[[1,1,0],[1,1,1],[0,1,1]], whose determinant
is−1. It is invertible, but its leading2-by-2 block is singular. This unpivoted
recurrence encounters exact denominator0 at order2 and cannot continue. The
analysis API explicitly returns solved=false, breakdown_order2 and only the last
completed one-element prefix; that partial vector is not a solution of the full
system. The ordinary solve throws. The separate pivoted dense solver succeeds on
the full original matrix.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/levinson_breakdown.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/linear_solvers.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> r{1, 1, 0}, b{1, 2, 3};
    auto t = dm::algorithms::levinson_trace(r, b);
    auto g =
        dm::algorithms::gaussian_elimination(dm::algorithms::symmetric_toeplitz_matrix(r),
                                             b);
    bool threw = false;
    try {
        auto x = dm::algorithms::levinson_solve(r, b);
    } catch (const std::domain_error &) {
        threw = true;
    }
    std::print("| actual Levinson solved | actual breakdown order | actual completed prefix length | actual " "failed denominator | ordinary solve threw | pivoted full solve completed |\n| --- | ---: | " "---: | ---: | --- | --- |\n| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
               t.analysis.solved, t.analysis.breakdown_order,
               t.analysis.solution.size(), t.attempts.back().denominator, threw,
               g.solved);
    std::print("\nActual pivoted full solution: {}, {}, {}.\n", g.x[0], g.x[1],
               g.x[2]);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("one", {0, 2},
           std::format("Actual completed prefix\norder{}", t.states.back().order)).node("fail", {6, 2}, std::format("Actual attempted order{}\ndenominator{}; stop", t.attempts.back().order, t.attempts.back().denominator)).node("dense", {6, -2}, std::format("Independent pivoted full solve\ncompleted{}; length{}", g.solved, g.x.size())).edge("one", "fail").save_svg("build/levinson_breakdown.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual Levinson solved | actual breakdown order | actual completed prefix length | actual failed denominator | ordinary solve threw | pivoted full solve completed |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | --- |
| false | 2 | 1 | 0 | true | true |

Actual pivoted full solution: -1, 2, 1.
\image(figures/levinson_breakdown.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual leading-block breakdown despite an invertible full matrix.)
\alttext(Only the order1 prefix is reported as complete; the actual failed order2 denominator and independent pivoted full solution keep algorithmic breakdown separate from matrix singularity.)

> Quadratic work and residual observations

At extension s→s+1, three error sums evaluate3*s scalar multiply/add terms and
three new vectors write3*(s+1) entries. Completing n orders therefore
evaluates3*n*(n−1)/2 error terms and3*((n*(n+1)/2)−1) extension entries, in
addition to initialization. These selected counters exclude validation,
allocation, capture and residual work. The following actual AR(1)-shaped rows
r_k=0.8^k are positive definite mathematically, but neither that fact nor small
observed residuals supplies a universal floating-point stability guarantee. Each
row is solved once in count-only mode; residuals are independent additional
diagnostics.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/levinson_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/levinson.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <cmath>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| original order | actual completed orders | actual error multiply-add terms | actual " "extension vector entries | actual widened residual norm | actual normwise backward-error " "ratio |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int n : {4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128}) {
        std::vector<double> r(n), b(n, 1);
        for (int k = 0; k < n; ++k)
            r[k] = std::pow(.8, k);
        auto a = dm::algorithms::levinson_analyze(r, b);
        auto d = dm::algorithms::levinson_residual(r, b, a.solution);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.12g} | {:.12g} |\n", n,
                   a.work.completed_orders, a.work.dot_products,
                   a.work.vector_updates, d.infinity_norm, d.backward_error);
        x.push_back(n);
        y.push_back(a.work.dot_products);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, y, "actual error terms", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, y, "",
                                                                {37, 99, 235}, 5).title("Actual quadratic recurrence work on explicit AR rows").x_label("original matrix order").y_label("actual error-sum multiply-add terms").save_svg("build/levinson_work.svg");
}
```
